/*
Original work, written for tests/fixtures/nonlinear-timeline (temporal-
consistency mechanism material, 2026-08-03). Parallel-action structure:
two friends crosscut across a phone call using MEANWHILE / SIMULTANEOUSLY
heading markers, per the markers extractTemporalConstraints() actually
parses. Temporally clean as written -- 0 expected contradictions.
*/

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

CALLIE ABARA, twenties, phone wedged between shoulder and ear, stirs a pot one-handed while flour dusts the counter behind her.

CALLIE
(into phone)
You're not seriously doing this the night before your interview.

INT. BEN'S CAR - PARKED OUTSIDE A BAR - NIGHT - MEANWHILE

MEANWHILE, across town: BEN OKONJO, twenties, sits in his car engine off, staring at a bar's neon sign through the windshield.

BEN
(into phone)
I'm not doing anything. I'm sitting in a parked car. Sitting is legal.

CALLIE
Sitting outside Danny's is a warning shot. I can hear the sign buzzing from here.

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Callie turns the stove down, wipes a hand on her jeans, phone still cradled.

CALLIE
Talk to me instead. What's actually going on.

INT. BEN'S CAR - PARKED OUTSIDE A BAR - NIGHT - SIMULTANEOUSLY

SIMULTANEOUSLY, Ben's hand hovers over the door handle, doesn't open it.

BEN
Nothing's going on. That's the problem. I've done everything right for four months and it feels like nothing at all.

CALLIE (V.O.)
(through phone, gentle)
So you're going to undo four months in one bad hour.

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Callie sets a timer on the oven, sits down at the counter properly now, giving the call her full attention.

CALLIE
Ben. Put your hand back on the wheel.

INT. BEN'S CAR - PARKED OUTSIDE A BAR - NIGHT

A beat. Ben's hand drops from the handle back to his lap.

BEN
It's back on my lap, which is close enough.

CALLIE (V.O.)
It's a start. What time's the interview.

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT - MEANWHILE

MEANWHILE, back in the kitchen, Callie pulls a stack of index cards from a drawer, flips through them one-handed while she talks.

CALLIE
Nine sharp. I made you cards. They're useless to you tonight but I made them.

INT. BEN'S CAR - PARKED OUTSIDE A BAR - NIGHT

Ben laughs, actually laughs, some of the tension draining out of his shoulders.

BEN
Read me one. Just one. Then I'm driving home, I promise.

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Callie pulls a card at random, squints at her own handwriting.

CALLIE
"Tell them about the shelter dog story. Not the sad part. The part where you stayed."

INT. BEN'S CAR - PARKED OUTSIDE A BAR - NIGHT

Ben starts the engine. The neon sign glows uselessly behind him now, already forgotten.

BEN
The part where I stayed. Yeah. I can do that part.

INT. CALLIE'S APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Callie smiles, relieved, resumes stirring the pot with her free hand.

CALLIE
Text me the second it's over. Good or bad, I want the first version, not the polished one.

INT. BEN'S CAR - MOVING - NIGHT

Ben pulls out of the parking spot, phone now on speaker on the passenger seat, the bar shrinking in his rearview mirror.

BEN
First version. Deal. Go finish whatever's about to boil over.

Behind him, the pot's lid rattles faintly through the phone's speaker, right on cue.
